## SoLoud::ChannelMixer


The SoLoud::ChannelMixer is a composable audio source that wraps any other
audio source and converts its speaker layout into a different layout through
an explicit, user-editable channel mapping matrix. It can optionally resample
the wrapped stream to a different output sample rate at the same time. The
output layout and the output sample rate are applied by a single
`configure()` call, so a layout switch and a rate switch form one atomic
configuration transaction.

Typical use cases are desktop players that have to feed different output
layouts: folding stereo material down to mono without halving its perceived
level, or playing 5.1 material on stereo speakers without leaking the
low-frequency effects (LFE) channel into the left and right speakers.

The wrapped source is not owned by the mixer; as with every other SoLoud
source reference, the caller keeps the source object alive for as long as the
mixer may play.

### Channel layouts


The mixer accepts the same speaker layouts the engine itself supports,
identified by channel count: 1 (mono), 2 (stereo), 4 (quad), 6 (5.1) and
8 (7.1). The channel order inside the multi-channel layouts is the one
documented in soloud.h:

|Index|5.1 / 7.1 speaker|
|:----|:----------------|
|0|Front left|
|1|Front right|
|2|Front center|
|3|LFE|
|4|Back left|
|5|Back right|
|6|Side left (7.1 only)|
|7|Side right (7.1 only)|

The `CHANNEL` enum in ChannelMixer names these indices
(`ChannelMixer::FRONT_LEFT`, `ChannelMixer::LFE`, and so on).

### Default mapping rules


`configure()` generates a default mapping matrix. Every coefficient is either
an identical-speaker pass-through at gain 1.0, the equal-power fold gain
`1/sqrt(2)`, or one of the coefficients already used by the engine's internal
`panAndExpand()` mixer (0.5, 0.3, 0.2, 0.7). Two rules are worth calling out:

- A stereo pair folded onto a single speaker is combined at equal power, so a
  stereo frame `(0.2, -0.4)` downmixes to `(0.2 + -0.4) / sqrt(2)`, i.e.
  `-0.141421`, rather than being averaged down to half volume.
- The LFE channel only ever feeds an LFE output. When the target layout has no
  LFE speaker (mono, stereo, quad), the LFE is dropped instead of being mixed
  into the full-range speakers, and no LFE is synthesized from full-range
  material when upmixing.

Original stereo material configured for stereo output at its native sample
rate passes through bit-exact: the wrapped source's samples are forwarded
verbatim with no matrix math and no resampling.

### ChannelMixer.configure()


Wraps a source and applies the whole configuration transaction in one call.

    // Play a 5.1 source on stereo speakers at the source's own rate.
    gMixer.configure(&gSurround, 2);

    // Fold a stereo source to mono and resample the output to 48000 Hz.
    gMixer.configure(&gMusic, 1, 48000.0f);

The parameters are validated before any state changes. An invalid layout
(anything other than 1, 2, 4, 6 or 8 channels), a null source without a
previously set source, or a negative/NaN sample rate makes the call return
`INVALID_PARAMETER` and leaves the current configuration, and any instances
already playing, untouched. A sample rate of 0 (the default) means the mixer
follows the wrapped source's native rate.

### ChannelMixer.setSource()


Replaces only the wrapped source. The output layout, mapping matrix and
output sample rate stay in effect. The new source must produce the same
number of channels as the matrix expects.

    gMixer.setSource(&gNextSong); // same layout, new material

### ChannelMixer.setMapping(), ChannelMixer.getMapping()


Overwrites or reads one matrix coefficient. The convention is
`mMapping[dst][src]`: the gain of source channel `aSrcChannel` into output
channel `aDstChannel`. This is what makes the source composable - any
coefficient, including the LFE routing, can be customized.

    // Mute the front center in a 5.1 -> 5.1 pass-through.
    gMixer.setMapping(SoLoud::ChannelMixer::FRONT_CENTER,
                      SoLoud::ChannelMixer::FRONT_CENTER, 0.0f);

Out-of-range channel indices return `INVALID_PARAMETER`.

### ChannelMixer.resetMapping()


Regenerates the default mapping matrix for the current source and output
layouts, discarding any custom coefficients set with `setMapping()`.

    gMixer.resetMapping();

### ChannelMixer.getSourceChannels(), ChannelMixer.getOutputSamplerate()


Return the wrapped source's channel count and the mixer's effective output
sample rate. When `configure()` was called with rate 0, the reported rate is
the wrapped source's native rate.

### ChannelMixer.setLooping(), ChannelMixer.setLoopPoint(), ChannelMixer.getLoopPoint()


Adjusting the looping of a channel mixer has no effect; loop behavior is
controlled by the wrapped source.

\pagebreak
### ChannelMixer.setFilter()


As with any other audio source, you can attach filters to a channel mixer.

    gMixer.setFilter(0, &gLofi);

### ChannelMixer.stop()


You can stop all instances of a channel mixer with stop(). This is equivalent
of calling soloud.stopAudioSource() with the sound source.

    gMixer.stop();

### ChannelMixer.setInaudibleBehavior()

Set the inaudible behavior of the sound. By default, if a sound is
inaudible, it's paused, and will resume when it becomes audible again.
With this function you can tell SoLoud to either kill the sound if it
becomes inaudible, or to keep ticking the sound even if it's
inaudible.

	// Keep mixing even when nothing is heard.
	gMixer.setInaudibleBehavior(true, false);

### ChannelMixer.setVolume()

Set the default volume of the instances created from this audio source.

    gMixer.setVolume(0.9f);

### Inherited 3d audio interfaces

Like all other audio sources, the channel mixer inherits the 3d audio interfaces. Please refer to the 3d audio chapter for details on:

- ChannelMixer.set3dMinMaxDistance()
- ChannelMixer.set3dAttenuation()
- ChannelMixer.set3dDopplerFactor()
- ChannelMixer.set3dProcessing()
- ChannelMixer.set3dListenerRelative()
- ChannelMixer.set3dDistanceDelay()
- ChannelMixer.set3dCollider()
- ChannelMixer.set3dAttenuator()
